// CONFORMANCE: deferred
// Conformance: `catch (e: DbError.UniqueViolation)` must catch ONLY the
// specific subtype — sibling subtypes (`DbError.ForeignKeyViolation`)
// and other parents (`HttpError`) must not match. The classifier
// already maps PG SQLSTATE 23505 to `"DbError.UniqueViolation"`
// (Sprint 3A landed in `runner::mod.rs::classify_pg_error`); what's
// still pending is the parser side.
//
// DEFERRED RATIONALE
// ------------------
// Sprint 3B owns the parser change for dotted type names in
// `catch (e: T.Sub)`. As of HEAD `72f7049` the parser in
// `src/parser/stmt.rs::parse_try_stmt` reads ONE ident after `:`:
//
//     let catch_type = if self.check_symbol(':') {
//         self.expect_symbol(':')?;
//         Some(self.expect_ident("expected error type after ':'")?)
//     } else { None };
//
// so the literal source `catch (e: DbError.UniqueViolation)` fails to
// parse — the `.` is consumed as a postfix operator on the ident, not
// as part of the type name. Once Sprint 3B extends `expect_ident` (or
// adds a `parse_qualified_type`) to accept the dotted form, drop the
// deferred marker. The classifier and the matcher are both already
// ready — see `JWC_ERROR_KINDS` and `catch_type_matches`.
//
// The test also needs an error source classified as the specific
// subtype, which today requires either a real Postgres connection
// (testcontainers) or the same kind of no-DB classifier hook discussed
// in `case_typed_catch_db_error.jwc`. Plan: combine the two unlocks in
// one follow-up sprint.
function main() {
    try {
        // Future: a builtin that triggers SQLSTATE 23505 against a real
        // table. The expected stdout below reflects the post-unlock
        // contract.
        let _ignored = raw_sql("insert into users(id) values (1)");
        let _dup = raw_sql("insert into users(id) values (1)");
        print("never");
    } catch (e: DbError.UniqueViolation) {
        print("caught unique violation");
    }
}
